WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.570
Now we're going to talk about my good friends Jez and JCL.

2
00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:13.570
If you're keeping track of acronyms at home, that's Job Entry Subsystem and Job Control Language.

3
00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:18.570
Jez is a component of ZOS that's responsible for managing batch workloads.

4
00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.570
In other words, you've got this big pile of work that needs to get done.

5
00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:28.570
And Jez manages that pile of work, including receiving the jobs, making sure they make sense,

6
00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:33.570
putting all the pieces together, scheduling them to run, getting them into the pipeline,

7
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:38.570
and making sure that when they run, the output goes to the right place.

8
00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:46.570
JCL is a scripting language that we use to instruct the system how we want it to run those jobs once JCL is ready to run them.

9
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:51.570
Now there are two flavors of Jez, Jez 2 and Jez 3.

10
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:58.570
They handle things a little bit differently, enough for them to be a Jez 2 and a Jez 3, and most people use Jez 2.

11
00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:04.570
But the fundamentals are the same. They both accept and queue jobs submitted for execution.

12
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:08.570
They both have the concept of an input and an output queue.

13
00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:13.570
They both queue jobs for an initiator. That's a Jez program that goes around and says,

14
00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.570
hey, give me the next job. We're ready to do some work.

15
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:22.570
And there's also this concept of accepting output from a job when it runs and managing where it goes.

16
00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:29.570
It might go to a printer, it might get deleted, or maybe it's saved on a spool for an output manager to retrieve.

17
00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:39.570
The term spool is probably a new one. You can just think of it as a method of handling data where new data can be fed in while existing data is pulled off.

18
00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.570
So a spool functions both as an input and an output for Jez.

19
00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.570
Some of the jobs that get run on the ZOS are what you call batch jobs.

20
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:56.570
That term comes from the way that they're started, typically with a single job that ends up doing a whole lot of work with minimal human interaction,

21
00:01:56.570 --> 00:02:01.570
and they're either scheduled for a specific time or on an as-needed basis.

22
00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:08.570
So an example of a batch job might be a job that looks at the inventory of a warehouse and the orders that come in during the day,

23
00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:13.570
and determines how the truck should be loaded up overnight for when the drivers show up the next morning.

24
00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:21.570
You would just make sure that the job is pointing at the right data, run it, and know that the output will be where you expect it to be the next day.

25
00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:29.570
To speed up processing and make things run faster, jobs with similar needs get batched together and run as a group.

26
00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:32.570
No need for everyone to drive if you can carpool, right?

27
00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:41.570
Batch jobs can also be stored up during working hours and then executed in the evening when there aren't a lot of people logged in doing interactive work.

28
00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:47.570
Once a batch job begins, it continues until it's done or a severe error occurs.

29
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:54.570
After a batch job is submitted for execution, there's normally no further interaction required from you until it's done,

30
00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.570
but you typically watch it for a little while just to make sure nothing obviously wrong is happening.

31
00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:03.570
We've spent all this time talking about JES. Let's get over to JCL.

32
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:08.570
JCL is where you specify any resources that are required by the program.

33
00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:14.570
Let's say you're running a program that's doing something with a dataset like most jobs are.

34
00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:19.570
You obviously need to at least specify an input dataset and probably an output dataset.

35
00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:24.570
It also acts as a communicator between the operating system and the program.

36
00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:28.570
It makes jobs out of work that can run without any manual intervention,

37
00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:34.570
and it works according to the priority levels of each job because that's a thing you can set, too.

38
00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:40.570
In the JCL, you specify who you are, which is important for security reasons,

39
00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:45.570
and which resources and services are needed for the program to run.

40
00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:51.570
Safe to say, without JCL, programs would have a really hard time running on a mainframe.

41
00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:58.570
A job is a collection of instructions that performs a specific task when executed by the system.

42
00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:04.570
Think of a job as a unit of work, which can be made up of many job steps.

43
00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:11.570
Each job step is specified in JCL through a set of job control statements.

44
00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:17.570
The operating system uses the job entry subsystem to receive jobs into the operating system

45
00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:22.570
and to schedule them for processing, as well as handling the output.

46
00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:25.570
Here's how it goes from beginning to end.

47
00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:30.570
First, the user creates the JCL for the job, and just a little bit of insider information,

48
00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.570
very few people sit down and say, I'm going to write some JCL from scratch today.

49
00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:42.570
No, they have a few basic templates that they keep around and edit them to fit whatever purpose they have at that particular moment.

50
00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.570
Anyway, whether it's written from scratch or modified from something they had before,

51
00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.570
the user has the JCL they want to run.

52
00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:54.570
Next, the user submits the JCL to JES, the job entry subsystem.

53
00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.570
Now begins the job conversion.

54
00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.570
The JCL gets read by JES and stores that in the spool.

55
00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:06.570
All throughout this, the system communicates with the user on the status of their job.

56
00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:13.570
When the job is complete, the allocated resource and the space that it took up on the JES spool are released.

57
00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:16.570
Now the user can view and interpret the output.

58
00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.570
Let's take a look at what JCL looks like.

59
00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.570
This is a sample job card.

60
00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:26.570
First, you see the information required for the spooling of the job, where you want the job to go and run.

61
00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.570
Then there's the slash slash star statement.

62
00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:33.570
When you want to insert a space or a comment, prepend it with this.

63
00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.570
Next is the step and the name of the program you want to execute.

64
00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:42.570
In this case, the step is step 10, and the program is sort.

65
00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:47.570
For sort in and sort out, those DD statements are the data definitions,

66
00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:52.570
and they spell out the input and output data sets that the sort job needs to run.

67
00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:58.570
Since the sort out data set is new, we need to specify the details for it.

68
00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:04.570
We also have a few DD statements down here, which tells JES what to do with the output of the job itself.

69
00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:09.570
Sysin DD Asterik, which some people call a splat.

70
00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:13.570
This is the area where we send additional commands to the program.

71
00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:20.570
For example, maybe this sort program has a way of sorting things backwards or putting numbers before or after letters.

72
00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:23.570
Those might be parameters we'd set in here.

73
00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:30.570
To mark the end of the Sysin stream data, we use slash asterik, splat.

74
00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:36.570
Now, that's the basics of JCL, and if that made sense, you've got a good foundation to go much further.

75
00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:42.570
We aren't going to get into all the features of JCL, otherwise this will be a Coursera course on JCL.

76
00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:45.570
But there are a few other things I want you to know.

77
00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:52.570
You know what? We're actually running out of time for this video, and we need to continue it on to the next one.

78
00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:55.570
That's a bit of a spoiler for part two.
